#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "f1021ccd-e37f-5880-baf2-0f0eacd7dd95" :question/math-academy-id "e-481" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{{\\int}} \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2} \\right)\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin {2\\theta} =2\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin(2\\theta) = \\sin{\\theta}\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta= \\dfrac{x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\sin \\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right) &= \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2} \\right)\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin x\t&= \\sin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\cos  \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right).\n  }\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\sin {x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\left( - \\cos {x}\\right )+C \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cos {x}+C.\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096666 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "99b7b82d-431f-5992-9b1e-a80a6b3850c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-482" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\cos^2 x  \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\cos^2{x}$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2 x -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 2x +1 \\right).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\cos^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int  \\dfrac{1}{2} \\left(\\cos 2x +1 \\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int ( \\cos {2x} +1) \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n                                                                 &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {2x} +x\\right) + C\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin {2x} +\\dfrac{1}{2}x + C.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096473 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "6269e793-cee7-59d6-8656-12a5a7475f2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-483" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sin^2(3x)  \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\sin^2$ term: \n\n$$\n\\cos {2\\theta}=1-2\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {2\\theta} \\right )=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {6x} \\right )=\\sin^2(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096474 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "064d3c62-5cfc-502b-aba4-48e40042593f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "55a54d74-449e-5720-b808-9f275b678b2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-484" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta -\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos 2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x = \\cos 6x,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t{\\int} \\left(\\cos^2 3x - \\sin^2 3x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int  \\cos 6x\\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{6}\\sin6x+ C.\\\n                                                                \n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "064d3c62-5cfc-502b-aba4-48e40042593f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0f1116-d915-5ec7-81c7-f4086a52153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436648} {:answer/id #uuid "117955a3-da81-5764-9c65-84ef3f86d82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436649} {:answer/id #uuid "a73a5c77-6d93-5e05-ae26-4069ce27113c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436659} {:answer/id #uuid "c453cd7a-bf5c-5377-a103-509b529efe7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436660} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0f2c74-19ed-5e9c-96ae-282dcffd9a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc0f2c74-19ed-5e9c-96ae-282dcffd9a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436662} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b91902-f580-5044-b5d2-4725b9ece1f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63aa6d59-1b3d-5a26-a1f9-94507bee325e" :question/math-academy-id "q-10558" :question/problem "$\\int (x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x+{cos}^{2}⁡3x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x={cos}^{2}⁡3x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x+{cos}^{2}⁡3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (x+{cos}^{2}⁡3x-{sin}^{2}⁡3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453ab082-2bbc-52d2-9772-336a7f8f1822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436653} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8e8a0c-fae0-5beb-99c7-7f6e5e2eba40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436654} {:answer/id #uuid "846b5800-afa9-5986-857f-46a79c9e8c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436655} {:answer/id #uuid "91b53cb9-b208-5d9d-8a4b-ed0a7d3f6fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436658} {:answer/id #uuid "fafd609c-1308-5476-8673-122442085a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91b53cb9-b208-5d9d-8a4b-ed0a7d3f6fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436658} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ceb62bb-3be2-57db-812f-e1a858ecdd5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f11e7aad-252b-5e12-b641-681cfb746b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114680" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-[0-1] \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d522c0d-ad10-5b06-9fe3-2989a9dd3f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318102} {:answer/id #uuid "140301e9-e5d7-5ea2-aa4c-0f869d4dbf85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "140301e9-e5d7-5ea2-aa4c-0f869d4dbf85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436650} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d38f5c3-8667-50d7-9b7a-1d44eaf041bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06eac076-500f-5f71-9fa2-51a0ac0ba8f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-347169" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ so letting $\\theta =3x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =1-5{sin}^{2}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x))={sin}^{2}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(1-5{sin}^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,|}_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3})-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}]-[\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}] \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}]-[0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}] \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096666 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af67922d-f985-5063-b19c-20d087c931b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318158} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4f061a-ff83-54a7-966d-5615c8fefe75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e4f061a-ff83-54a7-966d-5615c8fefe75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436651} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ce878db-88ef-5b01-8a34-4af432d272f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6626a560-3774-509b-81a9-784bc902ebe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-347179" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b163cd4cc1add41ae2c043b4b3d7d818dd9b23de0a4b762bc85d348c0bca4def.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=3{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\pi $ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(3{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2x)\\,|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot (-\\pi ))+2(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(0+\\pi )-(0-2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\pi +2\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339e926b-935f-5ee2-947e-fc63fa89e95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339e926b-935f-5ee2-947e-fc63fa89e95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436652} :answer-field/id #uuid "06176425-884c-5dd3-8a01-9f1d8d2b6773" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2450f5df-4d67-52ba-8389-fdbd1502ce3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-348699" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}sin\\,(\\frac{x}{6})cos\\,(\\frac{x}{6})\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "064d3c62-5cfc-502b-aba4-48e40042593f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c02620-7e53-55de-8e0b-55604f5063bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317895} {:answer/id #uuid "878d0ada-2dc4-521b-bbd5-3ae173141b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878d0ada-2dc4-521b-bbd5-3ae173141b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436656} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb2c011-e2af-5007-a519-6733d9d7f018" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c12a6bf-0854-5c5c-9be3-55c25edc3abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-348727" :question/problem "![](images/08/0817984c94a989cc5d0801c829e9c0479c7afb30223450faea46ec486b4494c6.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+4x+7,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+4x+7)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(-5{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})+4x+7)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4x+7)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{2}+7x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =[-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(0)^{2}+7\\cdot 0]-[-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+7(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =0-[5+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-\\frac{7\\pi }{2}] \\\\  & =-5-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+\\frac{7\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096666 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Cosine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2da0af-70e4-5430-8430-baeb3a4118a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2da0af-70e4-5430-8430-baeb3a4118a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5275b8a3-a8ed-51bd-8426-91952cbec909" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfe0bdd7-dbe2-5dd0-8dfa-8c8cabd2a658" :question/math-academy-id "q-361712" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a71e9121e38cfa60bfac0864ee2686cad756e268b78b512f55367bf6af0f8c94.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+({sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{3}{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]-[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot 0+\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]-[\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot 0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc56d0d-1fc1-55b0-a858-0f72c1814fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339244} {:answer/id #uuid "222afa10-e65e-535c-b778-59ff5f57e53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339420} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab24c23-c3cd-5916-8d10-f8f9cd41df90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339654} {:answer/id #uuid "5240e2fd-ff1b-54d0-9ace-5be02e2c6a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339885} {:answer/id #uuid "839765cf-3921-5bbd-be4f-c72628e60a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "839765cf-3921-5bbd-be4f-c72628e60a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340311} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e95e6b-57c0-5f5a-83f7-9b72881b2c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ded7813f-7862-5c34-8d67-a2dd0cf18a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-74412" :question/problem "$\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}))\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}))\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
